<!-- 01 INTRO -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Coyote service</p>
    <h2>Coyote's RapidSear infrared and lit-knob electronics are what we see fail.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Coyote grill fails at an LA home, our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair trace it to a few patterns: an ignition electrode out on one zone, the RapidSear infrared burner failing on an S-Series grill while the main burners still cook, an LED-lit knob or interior light out on its own circuit, or a salt-corroded burner on a coastal install. These are the C-Series and S-Series built-ins sitting in BBQ islands across the seven counties, and coastal salt sets the corrosion clock. $89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p class="intro-speakable">Coyote Outdoor Living is an American outdoor kitchen brand that lands as mid-premium in our Southern California territory. Owners choose Coyote for the built-in look and the S-Series features, the RapidSear infrared burner, the illuminated control knobs, and the interior grill lights, at a price below the ultra-premium built-ins. That mix of gas hardware and lit electronics is exactly what shapes our Coyote service: we keep the burner-and-ignition diagnosis separate from the LED-and-wiring diagnosis so each gets fixed correctly.</p>

    <p>Our techs service the Coyote gas lineup: the value C-Series (model codes like C3C30, C3C34, and the C3CH, C3CL, and C3CO built-in variants) and the S-Series (C3SL30, C3SL36, C3SL42) with the RapidSear infrared burner and lit knobs. Widths run 30, 36, 42, and 50 inches, in natural gas and liquid propane.</p>

    <p>BHGS #A49573. Same-day across LA, Orange County, Ventura. Common Coyote burners and electrodes on the van.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 COYOTE SERIES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-number">Series and architecture</p>
    <h2>Coyote series and what fails on each.</h2>

    <p class="lead">Coyote gas grills split into two lines. The C-Series is the value built-in, and the S-Series adds the RapidSear infrared burner plus lit electronics. We identify by the full model code before ordering, because the S-Series carries parts the C-Series does not.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">C-Series built-in grills</h3>
    <p>The value Coyote built-in. Standard tube burners, electronic ignition, sized across the lineup, for example C3C30 and C3C34, plus the C3CH36, C3CL36, C3CL42, and C3CO50 built-in variants. Common C-Series failures are ignition electrode wear, burner corrosion on coastal installs, and valve seizure after seasonal inactivity.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">S-Series grills (RapidSear infrared, lit knobs)</h3>
    <p>The S-Series (C3SL30, C3SL36, C3SL42) is the step-up line. It adds a RapidSear infrared burner for high-heat searing, illuminated control knobs, and interior grill lights. Two service realities follow: the RapidSear infrared burner runs hotter and fails earlier than the tube burners, and the lit knobs and interior lights are an electrical circuit separate from the gas system. We diagnose those two systems independently.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">RapidSear infrared burner</h3>
    <p>The S-Series RapidSear burner is a high-heat infrared searing burner. It cycles harder than the main burners and fails earlier, usually the ceramic element or the ignition feeding it. When a Coyote sear zone quits while the main grill works, we diagnose the element, orifice, and ignition separately, then replace. It is a different part than a standard burner.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Ignition and lit electronics</h3>
    <p>Coyote uses electronic ignition across the line; a single dead zone is usually that burner's electrode. Separately, the S-Series illuminated knobs and interior lights run on their own LED circuit, which fails at the LED and wiring stage. Keeping the two diagnoses separate is what stops a simple light fault from being misread as a bigger problem.</p>

    <h3 class="series-h3">Burners, grates, regulator</h3>
    <p><strong>Burners:</strong> stainless tube burners, corrosion-prone on coastal installs, replaced per zone before a pinhole damages the manifold. <strong>Cooking grates:</strong> stainless grates that rust on the coast, replaced as a set. <strong>Regulator:</strong> low flame across zones on LP setups points at the regulator.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 TYPICAL SCENARIOS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Common scenarios</p>
    <h2>Top Coyote repairs in our 5-county territory.</h2>

    <p>Ranked by how often our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair see them, most common first.</p>

    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Ignition electrode failure.</strong> Single zone won't light, others working. Test and replace the electrode for that burner.</li>
      <li><strong>RapidSear infrared burner failure (S-Series).</strong> Sear zone dead, main grill fine. Diagnose the infrared element, orifice, and ignition, then replace.</li>
      <li><strong>Lit knob or interior light out (S-Series).</strong> LED and wiring fault on the lighting circuit, separate from the gas system.</li>
      <li><strong>Coastal burner corrosion.</strong> Salt-driven pinhole on Malibu and Newport Beach installs. Per-zone replacement before manifold damage.</li>
      <li><strong>Valve seizure after seasonal inactivity.</strong> Knob won't turn or won't hold flame. Disassemble, clean, lubricate, occasionally replace.</li>
      <li><strong>Regulator drop.</strong> Low flame across all zones on LP setups. Regulator check and replacement.</li>
      <li><strong>Cooking grate corrosion.</strong> Stainless grates rust through on coastal grills. Replacement set.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-grill no-spark.</strong> Ignition module or battery and wiring on units that use one.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 REAL REPAIR STORIES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-number">Real repairs</p>
    <h2>Composite stories from the route.</h2>

    <p class="lead">Four Coyote jobs typical of the past 90 days. Models, symptoms, diagnosis, time, total. Names omitted; the scenarios are what we see weekly.</p>

    <div class="story-grid">
      <div class="story-card">
        <h3>Irvine, Coyote C3SL36 S-Series (2019 install)</h3>
        <p>RapidSear infrared burner would not light, main burners fine. Diagnosis: infrared element failed plus a fouled orifice. Replaced the RapidSear burner and cleared the orifice, tested the sear ignition. Total: $89 plus $210 part plus 1 hour = $359.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-card">
        <h3>Beverly Hills, Coyote C3SL30 S-Series (2020 install)</h3>
        <p>Two illuminated knobs and one interior light dead; grill cooked fine. Diagnosis: LED circuit fault at the knob assembly and a failed interior light module. Replaced the affected LED components, verified the lighting circuit. Total: $89 plus $130 parts plus 1 hour = $309.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-card">
        <h3>Newport Beach, Coyote C3C34 C-Series (2017 install)</h3>
        <p>One zone dead and a visible pinhole in an adjacent burner. Coastal salt at 8 years. Diagnosis: failed electrode plus an early burner pinhole. Replaced the electrode and the corroded burner before manifold damage. Total: $89 plus $240 parts plus 1.25 hours = $509.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-card">
        <h3>Pasadena, Coyote C3CL42 built-in (2015 install)</h3>
        <p>Multiple issues at 10 years: a corroded burner, rusted cook grates, and a sticking valve. Did all three in one visit. Total: $89 plus $420 parts plus 1.75 hours = $719. The alternative, a replacement built-in, was well over $3,000 installed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

